{#
sharing/_layout.html.twig
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
The chrome for the two pages a person with no account here ever sees: a shared
calendar, and a booking page.

A layout of its own rather than _layout/app.html.twig, and the reason is one
line in that file: it renders `csrf_token('ajax')` into a meta tag, which
starts a session. Extending it would mean a Set-Cookie on every fetch of a
public URL — one session per visitor, and one per poll of the .ics — which is
the cost SharedCalendarController's docblock says it will not pay. Nothing
below touches app.user, app.session or csrf_token, and nothing may be added
that does.

It is also deliberately anonymous. No sidebar, no account switcher, no
"plMail" branding beyond the small mark in the corner: the recipient was sent a
calendar, not introduced to somebody's mail client, and the page should not
tell them which install it came from or who else uses it.

── The theme is the owner's, and it is the only thing of theirs here ────────

`appearance` is a PublicAppearance: a class list, a theme name and a block of
CSS variables, built by PublicAppearanceResolver from the account the link
belongs to. That is what makes a shared calendar look like the calendar it came
out of rather than like the `:root` fallback, which is not a theme anybody
picked — it is what a stylesheet resolves against before a user is known.

It carries no name, no address and no id, and the object is three strings
precisely so that it cannot: the same argument SharedOccurrence makes about
events, applied to appearance. A page rendered without it falls back to Paper,
which is what a fresh account starts as.

The one script here resolves a `system` theme against the visitor's own OS. It
deliberately does NOT read localStorage the way _layout/app.html.twig does —
that cache holds the VISITOR's appearance, and replaying it would repaint
somebody else's calendar in the reader's colours.

Blocks:
  title            the browser tab
  body             the page
  container_class  the width the content is held to

Variables:
  appearance  PublicAppearance  the owner's theme, or Paper by default
#}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="{{ app.request.locale|replace({'_': '-'}) }}"
      class="{{ appearance is defined ? appearance.htmlClass : '' }}"
      data-theme="{{ appearance is defined ? appearance.theme : 'paper' }}"
      style="{{ appearance is defined ? appearance.cssVariables : '' }}">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover">
    <title>{% block title %}{{ 'calendar.share.page_title'|trans }}{% endblock %}</title>
    {% include '_partials/_favicon.html.twig' %}

    {# Not indexed, and not followed. A shared calendar is unlisted rather than
       secret only because the token is unguessable — a crawler that found one
       in a referrer header and published it would make the token pointless. #}
    <meta name="robots" content="noindex, nofollow">
    {# Same reason, from the other direction: a link clicked out of this page
       must not carry the token in the Referer header to wherever it goes. #}
    <meta name="referrer" content="no-referrer">

    <script>
        // A `system` theme is the one the server cannot resolve: it means
        // "whatever the machine reading this prefers", and only the machine
        // knows. Three lines, before first paint, exactly as the application's
        // own layout does it — without them a system-themed owner's page paints
        // light and then never corrects, because nothing else here is dynamic.
        if (document.documentElement.dataset.theme === 'system' &&
            window.matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').matches) {
            document.documentElement.classList.add('dark');
        }
    </script>

    {{ importmap('app') }}
</head>
<body class="min-h-screen app-bg text-ink antialiased">
    <main class="mx-auto w-full {% block container_class %}max-w-2xl{% endblock %} px-3 py-6 sm:px-4 sm:py-10">
        {% block body %}{% endblock %}
    </main>
</body>
</html>
